[38;5;231m⏺[39m Answer noted: hostname detection.

  [1mSecond question — how should [38;5;153mapp.js[39m get at the settings?[0m

  The fork: whether the settings module is a second plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m that sets a global, or a real ES module that [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  imports. This matters beyond style because it changes how you open the page. Today [38;5;153mindex.html[39m works by double-clicking
  it ([38;5;153mfile://[39m); ES modules are blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so that path would require serving the directory over a
  local HTTP server from then on.

  - [1mPlain script + global[0m — buys an exact match with the current pattern: add [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m,
    and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads a global. Nothing about how you open or run the page changes. Costs a global variable and
    load-order coupling (settings must come first or you get a runtime error). Cheap to convert to ESM later — it's one
    import line and two script tags.
  - [1mES modules[0m — buys a genuine module boundary: an explicit [38;5;153mimport[39m, no globals, no load-order trap, and the direction
    the rest of the ecosystem assumes. Costs the [38;5;153mfile://[39m workflow — you'd need [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar to open
    the app from then on — and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes to [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m.

  I recommend [1mplain script + global[0m. The repo's existing convention is plain scripts with no tooling, and the load-order
  risk is contained at two adjacent lines in one HTML file. Trading away the double-click workflow to remove one global
  isn't a good deal at this size — and since both options expose the same settings object shape, switching later
  touches three lines.

  I'm also assuming the Node side ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m) stays untouched — it's CommonJS, disconnected from the
  webapp, and has no API endpoint of its own. Say so if you'd rather the settings module serve both.
